00:15:46,280 --> 00:15:50,760
Scott: But basically the person who made the show just got fired.

184
00:15:51,681 --> 00:15:58,580
Scott: Even though the show is even before it aired, it's not on D+ yet as already getting a second season.

185
00:15:58,922 --> 00:16:02,700
Scott: And they're talking about a third season because the hype is so big they can order.

186
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:04,180
Scott: Basically, they're feeling it right.

187
00:16:04,581 --> 00:16:05,886
Scott: They're already decided.

188
00:16:05,926 --> 00:16:09,500
Scott: this is a success, but the person who made the show was fired mysteriously.

189
00:16:10,765 --> 00:16:14,980
Scott: So that's a real, uh, this is a mystery now.

190
00:16:15,420 --> 00:16:21,340
Scott: Maybe they're doing that to like, maybe they're sort of doing that to like, you got to assume, I don't know.

191
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:24,819
Scott: I can't even imagine what would happen there.

192
00:16:25,501 --> 00:16:32,820
Rym: I mean, I can, I could do a lot of speculation that I'm not going to do, but yeah, it's easy to just make shit up.

193
00:16:33,401 --> 00:16:41,720
Rym: I mean, generally when someone suddenly gets fired from something that nobody expected him to be fired from at an inauspicious time,

194
00:16:42,640 --> 00:16:42,821
Scott: and

195
00:16:44,186 --> 00:16:53,900
Rym: no one will say anything, like there's no information from anywhere, usually it's either because there's a lawsuit of some kind or something bad happened behind the scenes.

196
00:16:54,462 --> 00:16:59,580
Rym: But at the same time, it could be like a completely unrelated personal thing that we have no business knowing.

197
00:17:03,421 --> 00:17:07,800
Scott: But yeah, that's a real question mark for everybody.

198
00:17:08,701 --> 00:17:11,979
Scott: But if you're excited about some X-Men's, here you go.

199
00:17:12,319 --> 00:17:26,839
Rym: I'm pretty excited about this because the last X-Men's that I watched that is like a TV serial was actually the 2009 Wolverine and the X-Men, which if you haven't seen it out there, I will say that it is one of the best X-Men's.

200
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:30,739
Rym: It is absolutely fantastic and I kind of want to watch it again at some point.

201
00:17:31,381 --> 00:17:38,399
Scott: No, you know, it was Spider-Man seasons, but the Spider-Man is heavily delayed, so it's X-Men seasons right now.

202
00:17:40,341 --> 00:17:46,720
Rym: The best part about Wolverine and the X-Men is the premise is Scott Summers is like missing, so Wolverine has to run the X-Men.

203
00:17:47,200 --> 00:17:51,979
Rym: And you might think, but Rym, Wolverine is like the least capable person to lead a team.

204
00:17:52,803 --> 00:17:54,379
Scott: It depends which Wolverine you're talking about.

205
00:17:54,580 --> 00:18:05,660
Rym: The Wolverine from Fox X-Men and X-Men '97, that Wolverine is not capable of managing anyone, let alone Wolverine.

206
00:18:07,602 --> 00:18:12,840
Rym: So the whole plot is Wolverine has to lead the X-Men because Professor X is gone and Scott Summers can't deal with it.

207
00:18:13,401 --> 00:18:14,268
Scott: Is Storm still there?

208
00:18:14,288 --> 00:18:15,458
Scott: Storm is way better.

209
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:16,743
Rym: Storm's around.

210
00:18:17,426 --> 00:18:21,020
Rym: There's a lot going on, but White Queen ends up playing a big part in that one.

211
00:18:21,700 --> 00:18:22,858
Scott: Yeah, the comic books do that too.

212
00:18:23,140 --> 00:18:25,527
Scott: All these shows and movies are really just picking pieces from the actual comics.

213
00:18:33,828 --> 00:18:35,380
Scott: They don't have any new ideas in them.

214
00:18:35,460 --> 00:18:44,720
Scott: If you've read every X-Men comic, then there is absolutely nothing new to be had from watching any animated or live action X-Men because you've seen it before.

215
00:18:45,201 --> 00:18:48,226
Rym: I haven't read a ton of X-Men, but I've read enough to where.

216
00:18:48,627 --> 00:18:54,378
Rym: when Dark Phoenix was clearly about to happen and any other X-Men thing, I just feel like, "Oh, they're doing Dark Phoenix again.

217
00:18:54,478 --> 00:18:54,759
Rym: Okay.".

218
00:18:56,282 --> 00:19:01,519
Scott: If anyone out there does, you decide, "Huh, I should read some X-Men," and you probably should.

219
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:15,200
Scott: The way to do it actually is the crappy but functional Marvel Unlimited app, which is... I forget how much it costs, but basically you can pay per year.

220
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:19,520
Scott: Let's see, what is the... I can't find the pricing on the internet.

221
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:20,385
Rym: I was thinking about it.

222
00:19:20,425 --> 00:19:21,772
Rym: I kind of want to refill my X-Men cup.

223
00:19:24,221 --> 00:19:36,640
Scott: Basically, if you pay for the Marvel Unlimited app and you have an iPad, I guess you could read on a phone, but it won't be that great, you can basically read most Marvel comics that have ever existed.

224
00:19:36,860 --> 00:19:37,222
Scott: Oh, here it is.

225
00:19:37,303 --> 00:19:39,135
Scott: Monthly, $10 a month, annual, $70 a year.

226
00:19:41,062 --> 00:19:43,780
Scott: How many Marvel comics can you read in a year for $70?

227
00:19:43,780 --> 00:19:47,460
Scott: Considering that one floppy-ass comic book is going to cost you several dollars.

228
00:19:48,402 --> 00:19:55,240
Scott: It's like if you're buying more than 11 comics a year, you're getting almost the entire history of Marvel comics with this thing.

229
00:19:56,760 --> 00:20:11,080
Scott: You can go there and basically read Uncanny X-Men from the beginning for way cheaper than buying even the cheap combined volumes or whatever in full color, high res on your thing.

230
00:20:11,160 --> 00:20:13,419
Scott: It's just navigating the app really sucks.

231
00:20:14,540 --> 00:20:15,598
Rym: Yeah, I can imagine that.

232
00:20:16,022 --> 00:20:19,060
Scott: It's not a very good app, but reading comics in it functions.

233
00:20:22,192 --> 00:20:25,070
Rym: But anyway, things of the day!

234
00:20:26,010 --> 00:20:30,050
Rym: I think Scott found this a little while ago and can't say how long it'll stay up.

235
00:20:30,190 --> 00:20:47,033
Rym: This YouTube video has less than 600 views and was uploaded two months ago, but it's a very important YouTube video because it is the entirety of a movie that we used to talk about and reference a lot on GeekNights like back in 2006, 2005.

236
00:20:47,033 --> 00:20:51,950
Rym: And it's a movie that we reference a lot in our daily lives to this day that we saw in college.

237
00:20:52,471 --> 00:21:01,330
Rym: And this movie is the first Korean movie I ever saw, like way before Cool Korea and like the modern K-pop invasion and K-drama.

238
00:21:01,410 --> 00:21:04,189
Scott: I might have seen a Korean animated movie before I saw this.

239
00:21:05,172 --> 00:21:07,090
Rym: I'm not sure, I'd have to think back.

240
00:21:07,410 --> 00:21:09,590
Scott: There was one we saw, I forget what the name of it was.

241
00:21:09,852 --> 00:21:12,690
Scott: But I don't remember if it was before or after Attack the Gas Station.

242
00:21:12,990 --> 00:21:24,770
Rym: But we watched this movie and Attack the Gas Station remains just one of my favorite comedy movies for a lot of reasons that we don't really have time to get into right now.

243
00:21:24,850 --> 00:21:26,570
Rym: I'm pretty sure we've reviewed it on GeekNights.

244
00:21:27,190 --> 00:21:29,070
Scott: We've definitely talked about this on GeekNights a lot.

245
00:21:29,610 --> 00:21:40,650
Rym: Yeah, but if you've never seen it, Attack the Gas Station is a movie about a bunch of delinquents who rob a gas station comedically and then they're hanging out like in a convenience store eating some noodles and they're bored.

246
00:21:40,930 --> 00:21:41,684
Rym: Not sure what to do.

247
00:21:42,476 --> 00:21:43,690
Scott: Across the street from the gas station.

248
00:21:43,850 --> 00:21:44,447
Rym: Yeah, like right there.

249
00:21:45,051 --> 00:21:48,668
Rym: And then one of them just says, "Why don't we just rob the gas station

250
00:21:48,709 --> 00:21:48,950
Rym: again?".

251
00:21:49,971 --> 00:21:55,730
Rym: And that is where the movie starts, the second time they rob the same gas station in the same night.

252
00:21:56,792 --> 00:21:58,770
Scott: I think it was consecutive nights.

253
00:21:59,812 --> 00:22:01,566
Rym: I haven't seen this movie in at least 15 years.

254
00:22:02,990 --> 00:22:07,090
Scott: Yeah, and then basically the second robbery takes all night.

255
00:22:08,391 --> 00:22:16,343
Rym: Let's just say the second robbery encounters a few complications, ranging from the manager of the gas station saying, "What the fuck?

256
00:22:16,363 --> 00:22:17,285
Rym: You already robbed me.

257
00:22:17,345 --> 00:22:18,146
Rym: There's no money.

258
00:22:18,166 --> 00:22:18,787
Rym: What do you

259
00:22:18,847 --> 00:22:19,008
Rym: want?".

260
00:22:22,592 --> 00:22:24,141
Rym: And it escalates rapidly.

261
00:22:24,161 --> 00:22:25,629
Rym: This movie just really stuck with us.

262
00:22:26,090 --> 00:22:27,498
Rym: It's also really long.

263
00:22:27,518 --> 00:22:28,524
Rym: It's almost two hours long.

264
00:22:30,971 --> 00:22:31,995
Rym: I recommend this movie.

265
00:22:32,196 --> 00:22:36,370
Rym: I recommend you watch it on YouTube if you've never seen it before, before this video is taken down.

266
00:22:38,114 --> 00:22:38,642
Rym: So what do you got?

267
00:22:40,611 --> 00:22:46,870
Scott: So we all know that old adage about you can't fold a piece of paper in half more than so many times, right?

268
00:22:47,471 --> 00:22:50,849
Scott: It depends on the paper, obviously, what the exact limit is and whatnot.

269
00:22:51,833 --> 00:22:54,950
Scott: But it's like, yeah, because then the paper gets too thick and you can't fold it anymore.

270
00:22:55,650 --> 00:23:00,370
Scott: But what if there was a way to fold the paper that wasn't in half, right?

271
00:23:00,790 --> 00:23:04,950
Scott: Of course, there are many ways to fold paper, an infinite number of ways, actually.

272
00:23:06,130 --> 00:23:11,970
Rym: Well, not technically an infinite number because of the plank length and the size of an atom, but I digress.

273
00:23:12,470 --> 00:23:21,650
Scott: Regardless, if you have a piece of paper that is large and you want to get it into a small space, right?

274
00:23:22,091 --> 00:23:24,930
Scott: Say you've got, I don't know, a map, right?

275
00:23:25,090 --> 00:23:32,150
Scott: And you want to have like a big map of like a large area with great detail, but you don't want to carry around it unfolded, right?

276
00:23:32,170 --> 00:23:34,287
Scott: You want to squeeze it down so you can put it in your pocket.

277
00:23:34,871 --> 00:23:37,670
Scott: Usually you just fold it in half and sort of do a little brochure deal.

278
00:23:38,054 --> 00:23:39,409
Scott: And that usually is good enough.

279
00:23:39,852 --> 00:23:43,870
Scott: But what if there was a more efficient way to fold it into a small area?

280
00:23:45,032 --> 00:23:49,350
Scott: And what if you're in a case where you really need to fold it into a small area?

281
00:23:49,690 --> 00:23:53,650
Scott: Like maybe you're launching something into space, like, I don't know, a solar panel.

282
00:23:54,152 --> 00:23:55,804
Scott: And the solar panel is going to be big.

283
00:23:57,010 --> 00:24:02,950
Scott: So it captures a lot of sun, but you can't launch a big flat thing on your rocket, right?

284
00:24:03,191 --> 00:24:06,190
Scott: You need to fold that shit up, put it inside the rocket.

285
00:24:06,652 --> 00:24:09,450
Scott: And then once it gets up there, it unfolds, right?

286
00:24:10,711 --> 00:24:15,890
Scott: And so the way one way to do such a fold is called the Miura Ori fold.

287
00:24:16,291 --> 00:24:22,009
Scott: And here is a video on how to do this fold, which I think is really fun.

288
00:24:23,151 --> 00:24:31,330
Scott: And if you have large pieces of paper that you work with, blueprints, it could be fun to fold them in the Miura Ori and then file them without having to use folders.

289
00:24:32,411 --> 00:24:44,270
Scott: Or if you were, say, distributing a large brochure to people at maybe a booth and you folded all your brochures in the Miura Ori, and then people unfolded it and the brochure was a lot larger than they thought it was going to be.

290
00:24:45,396 --> 00:24:46,829
Scott: You could fit a lot of stuff on there.

291
00:24:47,350 --> 00:24:54,750
Scott: Obviously, it'll cost you more in ink and time because I don't think a machine, I don't know if a machine can do this fold for you if you're doing high quantities.

292
00:24:55,291 --> 00:25:05,670
Rym: I'll bet a machine could, but I also would bet because I went way back, way back when Scott and I used to try to print, we used to print a flyer for specific conventions because we used to do so many panels.

293
00:25:06,011 --> 00:25:09,070
Rym: So we'd print a flyer that had our panel scheduled for that convention.

294
00:25:09,632 --> 00:25:12,010
Rym: And I remember there were a lot of options for crazy folds.

295
00:25:12,710 --> 00:25:17,330
Rym: And depending on the number of folds, the price was exponentially more for the flyer.

296
00:25:18,011 --> 00:25:23,090
Rym: So I imagine you could get a specialized machine to do this, and I also imagine it would be so expensive, nobody would.

297
00:25:25,332 --> 00:25:28,230
Scott: Anyway, it's cool and useful to learn this fold, I guess.

298
00:25:29,011 --> 00:25:35,470
Scott: It's a practical origami and not a, I guess it's somewhat artistic, but it's not like you're making cranes and frogs and, you know.

299
00:25:35,854 --> 00:25:36,090
Scott: Yeah.

300
00:25:37,612 --> 00:25:40,870
Rym: In the meantime, the GeekNights Book Club book remains.

301
00:25:41,090 --> 00:25:41,779
Rym: The night is short.

302
00:25:41,880 --> 00:25:42,690
Rym: Walk on, girl.

303
00:25:42,891 --> 00:25:45,390
Rym: I imagine we'll do the show like within a few weeks after PAX.

304
00:25:45,911 --> 00:25:50,430
Rym: It's not a long read, but I expect we've both been really busy lately and I've been sick.

305
00:25:50,550 --> 00:25:54,930
Rym: So I will hazard that Scott has not read more of it since the last time we talked.

306
00:25:55,710 --> 00:25:55,831
Scott: Nope.

307
00:25:55,931 --> 00:25:58,568
Scott: I got a long train ride to PAX East to read.

308
00:25:59,150 --> 00:25:59,321
Rym: Yep.

309
00:26:00,430 --> 00:26:06,070
Rym: Speaking of PAX East, we will be live at PAX East with two brand new panels.

310
00:26:06,952 --> 00:26:09,510
Rym: And yeah, the slides are almost done for those panels.

311
00:26:09,670 --> 00:26:13,290
Rym: We're actually, we're catching up and we'll be ready and everything's going to be fine.

312
00:26:14,032 --> 00:26:21,670
Rym: Every game is the same game, which is the actual, for real, brand new academic serious talk that I think is going to be really good.

313
00:26:21,950 --> 00:26:28,170
Rym: I think it's one of those topics where the more we think about it, the more spicy stuff there is to talk about.

314
00:26:28,270 --> 00:26:30,509
Rym: I think it's a really good thing to explore in games.

315
00:26:31,110 --> 00:26:38,690
Rym: And the 39 games you must play, which is sort of the inversion of the 40 games you must play that we did a long time ago.

316
00:26:39,131 --> 00:26:48,528
Rym: Instead of 40 games that we have some idea as being like a representative set of all the different kinds of games to make you a well-rounded person or whatever.

317
00:26:48,568 --> 00:26:49,730
Rym: our shtick was for that panel.

318
00:26:50,290 --> 00:26:58,430
Rym: The shtick on this one is, here's 39 games that me and Scott specifically still have stuck in our craw, regardless of if they're good or if you would like them or if you could even play them.

319
00:26:59,011 --> 00:27:01,170
Scott: A self-aggrandizing panel.

320
00:27:02,291 --> 00:27:06,570
Rym: I think it'll be fun though, because as I started putting the list together, there's some good stuff to talk about.

321
00:27:07,334 --> 00:27:09,729
Rym: For example, Tokimeki Memorial on the SNES.

322
00:27:12,733 --> 00:27:16,450
Rym: So yeah, let's get into it because we have some stuff to do right after this.

323
00:27:16,770 --> 00:27:21,130
Rym: We went to, of course, the New York International Children's Film Festival, one of our favorite things.

324
00:27:21,130 --> 00:27:22,390
Scott: I'm going back there again on Saturday.

325
00:27:22,650 --> 00:27:24,168
Rym: Oh yeah, concierge.

326
00:27:25,630 --> 00:27:32,090
Rym: But the New York International Children's Film Festival is one of the best film festivals every year in New York City.

327
00:27:32,170 --> 00:27:36,370
Scott: It is the film festival that I have been to the most, and I have been to several.

328
00:27:37,193 --> 00:27:37,475
Rym: Yep.

329
00:27:37,495 --> 00:27:39,145
Rym: I look forward to it every year.

330
00:27:39,206 --> 00:27:39,870
Rym: It's one of those things.

331
00:27:40,151 --> 00:27:42,790
Scott: I sometimes forget when it's happening.

332
00:27:43,652 --> 00:27:47,049
Scott: Some years I remember and I go, and some years I'm like, "Oh, I missed

333
00:27:47,089 --> 00:27:47,130
Scott: it.".

334
00:27:47,751 --> 00:27:49,930
Rym: But it is just top notch.

335
00:27:50,250 --> 00:27:56,170
Rym: But one side effect is a lot of times, especially if there's a new anime movie that year, it's always in the festival.

336
00:27:56,290 --> 00:28:02,108
Rym: But two, the movies they pick for the festival are extremely well-curated.

337
00:28:02,228 --> 00:28:02,890
Rym: It's all hits.

338
00:28:03,630 --> 00:28:11,930
Scott: They basically get the best children's movies of the year and show them all together, plus a bunch of shorts that you could never see anywhere else easily, probably.

339
00:28:13,212 --> 00:28:14,296
Scott: And a lot of U.S.

340
00:28:14,336 --> 00:28:17,950
Scott: premieres of foreign ones, which is always the big deal.

341
00:28:18,971 --> 00:28:26,030
Scott: But it's like, hey, a lot of these are never going to screen anywhere or won't screen anywhere in the U.S.

342
00:28:27,372 --> 00:28:31,910
Scott: And so this is your only chance, and it's a good time.

343
00:28:32,490 --> 00:28:45,010
Rym: And one nuance that's part of the reason why the festival, I think, is so good and why we go to it so consistently is that the best children's movies are the ones that are equally enjoyable for the kids and the adults.

344
00:28:45,071 --> 00:28:47,249
Rym: So that is also one of the hardest kinds of movies to make.

345
00:28:48,551 --> 00:28:48,712
Scott: Yeah.

346
00:28:48,772 --> 00:28:54,290
Scott: If there was a movie that was like straight up children's movie, I don't think it would make the cut in the film festival.

347
00:28:54,750 --> 00:28:54,830
Rym: Yeah.

348
00:28:56,092 --> 00:29:11,270
Rym: So what we saw was a Spanish-French collaboration called Robot Dreams, which this movie is 102 minutes long, and it is one of those high concept, zero dialogue movies.

349
00:29:12,431 --> 00:29:13,974
Scott: Well, the comic book was right.

350
00:29:13,994 --> 00:29:21,450
Scott: So it's based on a comic book, which is published by first second, which I bought at the MOCA a very long time ago.

351
00:29:21,570 --> 00:29:22,996
Scott: When did the comic come out?

352
00:29:23,277 --> 00:29:24,140
Scott: Had to be at least 2007.

353
00:29:24,140 --> 00:29:24,241
Rym: Yeah.

354
00:29:26,750 --> 00:29:32,889
Scott: So I bought this comic in the early days of GeekNights and I had it on my shelf and I read it.

355
00:29:33,652 --> 00:29:36,163
Scott: And I remember clearly remember owning it.

356
00:29:36,404 --> 00:29:37,750
Scott: I clearly remember its existence.

357
00:29:38,653 --> 00:29:41,661
Scott: I can probably prove that I had it somehow.

358
00:29:42,022 --> 00:29:44,710
Rym: But do you remember the 21st night of September?

359
00:29:45,130 --> 00:29:45,250
Scott: Right.

360
00:29:45,310 --> 00:29:48,077
Scott: But I don't remember actually anything.

361
00:29:48,137 --> 00:29:54,050
Scott: I haven't read the comic in probably over a decade and I couldn't find it on my shelf anywhere.

362
00:29:54,712 --> 00:29:58,166
Scott: So I either gave it away or sold it or donated it or something.

363
00:29:59,430 --> 00:30:01,162
Rym: I think there's a 20% chance you read it.

364
00:30:03,551 --> 00:30:05,880
Scott: No, I definitely read it 100%.

365
00:30:05,880 --> 00:30:07,607
Scott: But I don't remember what happened in it.

366
00:30:08,109 --> 00:30:08,330
Scott: Right.

367
00:30:08,530 --> 00:30:10,297
Scott: Other than that, you know, what it looked like.

368
00:30:10,337 --> 00:30:13,409
Scott: There was a robot who had dreams and that's pretty much it.

369
00:30:15,275 --> 00:30:15,435
Rym: Yep.

370
00:30:15,455 --> 00:30:16,218
Rym: So this movie is great.

371
00:30:16,358 --> 00:30:18,385
Rym: Like I thoroughly enjoyed it.

372
00:30:18,486 --> 00:30:19,670
Rym: I would recommend everyone watch it.

373
00:30:19,730 --> 00:30:22,239
Rym: Like there's a lot going on in this movie.

374
00:30:23,062 --> 00:30:25,150
Rym: It's there's a lot we could say.

375
00:30:25,310 --> 00:30:31,810
Rym: But what I'm going to try to avoid is I have a feel like there's a lot of late create you can do and a lot of ways to interpret aspects of this movie.

376
00:30:32,312 --> 00:30:33,475
Rym: And it's a good movie.

377
00:30:33,496 --> 00:30:34,218
Scott: It's not that deep.

378
00:30:34,539 --> 00:30:37,790
Rym: No, it's not that deep, but there are multiple different layers of interpretation.

379
00:30:38,030 --> 00:30:44,750
Rym: However, because right before we saw it, Emily and I finished watching the Pluto anime.

380
00:30:44,910 --> 00:30:46,252
Rym: So Pluto was fresh in my mind.

381
00:30:46,272 --> 00:30:55,850
Rym: And that definitely colored this a little bit because in this universe, these robots are clearly sentient and yet clearly have zero rights.

382
00:30:58,692 --> 00:31:03,210
Rym: And that juxtaposition was a little bit jarring and added.

383
00:31:03,370 --> 00:31:04,135
Scott: I don't think that that was.

384
00:31:04,196 --> 00:31:06,250
Scott: that was just sort of not the point of it at all.

385
00:31:09,295 --> 00:31:16,610
Rym: The third darker reading of this is probably not intentional and not super relevant.

386
00:31:17,291 --> 00:31:18,856
Rym: And I'm going to try to avoid that.

387
00:31:18,916 --> 00:31:22,730
Rym: But I kept thinking back to Pluto the entire movie as a result.

388
00:31:24,052 --> 00:31:24,333
Scott: Yeah.

389
00:31:24,494 --> 00:31:27,144
Scott: So what happens in robot dreams is there is a dog.

390
00:31:27,225 --> 00:31:28,510
Scott: It's all animal people.

391
00:31:28,852 --> 00:31:32,229
Scott: But of course, there's also actual animal animals like the birds.

392
00:31:33,752 --> 00:31:36,300
Scott: But it does animal people and the animal.

393
00:31:36,661 --> 00:31:39,710
Scott: one animal dog who seems pretty cool is lonely.

394
00:31:40,472 --> 00:31:45,830
Scott: And so they buy a robot to be their friend and the robot is their friend.

395
00:31:45,990 --> 00:31:46,711
Scott: And it's awesome.

396
00:31:47,352 --> 00:32:01,290
Scott: But then due to sort of far fetched circumstances, the the the robot is separated from the dog and the robot has dreams during the separated period, even though they're a robot.

397
00:32:02,031 --> 00:32:02,996
Scott: And the dog is sad.

398
00:32:03,016 --> 00:32:06,189
Scott: And then, you know, things happen.

399
00:32:08,411 --> 00:32:12,398
Scott: What the story is about is obviously about loneliness, right?

400
00:32:12,699 --> 00:32:18,550
Scott: Friendship, moving on, you know, loss, trying to meet people.

401
00:32:19,130 --> 00:32:30,330
Scott: But the thing that really surprised me, which I don't know if it was in the book or not, because I remember the book, even though I read it, was that this is a super strong 80s New York period piece.

402
00:32:30,531 --> 00:32:35,690
Scott: Like it is more about New York than it is about the robots and the friendship and all that stuff.

403
00:32:35,890 --> 00:32:46,350
Rym: And it is like, as someone who's lived in New York for a long time now, it is more true to New York than a lot of things that very directly and intentionally try to be said in New York.

404
00:32:46,630 --> 00:32:55,030
Rym: Like the specific details it highlighted along the way were what I would say edge into inside New Yorker territory.

405
00:32:55,671 --> 00:32:55,831
Scott: Right.

406
00:32:55,852 --> 00:33:05,730
Scott: If you look at some other New York movies, right, you got your your taxi driver, your, you know, Dog Day Afternoon, your Ghostbusters, right?

407
00:33:05,910 --> 00:33:08,256
Scott: You're like extreme New York movies out there.

408
00:33:08,857 --> 00:33:14,270
Scott: But those movies are about what they're about more than they're about New York.

409
00:33:14,390 --> 00:33:20,350
Scott: They take place in New York and might be authentic to New York, but they're not necessarily about New York.

410
00:33:21,133 --> 00:33:24,490
Scott: Robot Dreams is at least 50 percent about New York.

411
00:33:24,730 --> 00:33:29,490
Rym: I would argue that Ghostbusters is the one exception where Ghostbusters is at least 20 percent about New York.

412
00:33:29,630 --> 00:33:31,893
Rym: I've read a lot of 20 percent, but it's not about.

413
00:33:31,933 --> 00:33:44,830
Rym: New York is specifically, effectively a character in Ghostbusters, partly due to the fact that New York is the one city in the world where literal hell rises up from the spirit realm and nobody gives a shit.

414
00:33:45,470 --> 00:33:49,130
Scott: But it's not as much about New York as Robot Dreams is.

415
00:33:49,471 --> 00:33:57,450
Rym: There is only one inaccurate moment in Robot Dreams, but it is inaccurate clearly intentionally about the 80s in New York.

416
00:33:58,650 --> 00:33:59,148
Rym: The Mets Yankees.

417
00:34:00,390 --> 00:34:00,711
Scott: Oh, yeah.

418
00:34:00,851 --> 00:34:01,754
Scott: That was that was in my.

419
00:34:02,034 --> 00:34:08,010
Scott: I did appreciate that the people who made this movie, first of all, set in the late 80s when the Mets were good and the Yankees were not as good.

420
00:34:08,530 --> 00:34:16,850
Scott: So but also I was so surprised not at the fact that it was a New York period piece, but that this is made by European people.

421
00:34:17,351 --> 00:34:17,732
Scott: I don't know.

422
00:34:17,831 --> 00:34:24,949
Scott: They must have been people who spent a lot of time in New York or had researched insanely because how else could they get so much right.

423
00:34:26,489 --> 00:34:27,694
Scott: And also they were.

424
00:34:28,376 --> 00:34:32,370
Scott: there is no doubt that they were Mets fans because basically, like all good people are.

425
00:34:33,011 --> 00:34:34,801
Scott: Yes, everything.

426
00:34:34,842 --> 00:34:35,606
Scott: They were 100 percent.

427
00:34:36,510 --> 00:34:39,889
Scott: They did not try to hide in any way that they were Mets fans.

428
00:34:39,969 --> 00:34:41,743
Scott: This movie was like, let's go, Mets.

429
00:34:41,824 --> 00:34:42,650
Scott: Fuck the Yankees.

430
00:34:43,710 --> 00:34:49,489
Scott: And without saying any words of dialog in the whole movie, they managed to say, let's go, Mets.

431
00:34:49,889 --> 00:34:50,011
Rym: Yeah.

432
00:34:50,813 --> 00:34:54,330
Rym: Not literally, but another character in the movie.

433
00:34:54,971 --> 00:34:55,431
Rym: Not really.

434
00:34:55,471 --> 00:35:06,350
Rym: It's more like a motif, but it is like it is so strong and central to everything that's going on in this movie is the Earth, Wind and Fire song September.

435
00:35:07,211 --> 00:35:07,492
Scott: Yes.

436
00:35:07,672 --> 00:35:13,189
Scott: So already that's a big song because of, you know, the guy who did all the videos.

437
00:35:13,530 --> 00:35:19,710
Rym: In the late 70s, when that song appeared on Earth through the 80s and even continuing into today.

438
00:35:19,790 --> 00:35:21,155
Rym: But that's a separate conversation.

439
00:35:21,677 --> 00:35:25,430
Rym: That song was big, like 78 through like 1984.

440
00:35:25,430 --> 00:35:26,630
Rym: The song was like still in the charts.

441
00:35:27,370 --> 00:35:29,030
Scott: It's a legendary hit song.

442
00:35:29,250 --> 00:35:29,890
Rym: It's one of the best songs.

443
00:35:31,270 --> 00:35:48,050
Scott: So and so I guess I was surprised in this movie at how much a actual licensed copyrighted trademarked things appeared that they must have either paid for or didn't care about legal consequences or there are no legal consequences.

444
00:35:48,732 --> 00:35:52,010
Scott: But they definitely paid for this song right to be used.

445
00:35:52,210 --> 00:35:54,370
Rym: Well, they played it like 30 times in the movie.

446
00:35:54,730 --> 00:35:55,541
Scott: They played it like 100 times.

447
00:35:56,953 --> 00:35:58,587
Scott: So they paid for it once and they played it.

448
00:35:59,753 --> 00:36:00,601
Scott: There's no dialogues.

449
00:36:00,642 --> 00:36:01,430
Scott: They got to do something.

450
00:36:01,650 --> 00:36:05,342
Scott: I guess there is dialogues because there's the lyrics of the song being sung as they play it.

451
00:36:08,690 --> 00:36:10,060
Rym: Well, you've also got the motifs of like.

452
00:36:10,241 --> 00:36:11,430
Scott: you hear Maurice White's voice.

453
00:36:11,590 --> 00:36:12,544
Scott: That's the only voice you hear.

454
00:36:13,131 --> 00:36:17,189
Rym: You hear Dog make a few grunts and exclamation noises at various points.

455
00:36:19,550 --> 00:36:22,910
Scott: Yeah, but there were also like in the image, right?

456
00:36:23,030 --> 00:36:26,150
Scott: It's like they're just like it just says Mets, Yankees, right?

457
00:36:26,310 --> 00:36:28,475
Scott: You know, there were some brands.

458
00:36:28,535 --> 00:36:35,670
Rym: I think there might have been like there were a handful of brands that were fakie versions, but most of them were actual things from that.

459
00:36:36,070 --> 00:36:40,070
Scott: It was literally just like Yankee Stadium, Mets, Mets, right?

460
00:36:40,150 --> 00:36:41,929
Scott: It's just like they're not hiding anything.

461
00:36:43,253 --> 00:36:45,248
Scott: And you it's I'm like, did they pay for that too?

462
00:36:45,875 --> 00:36:46,308
Scott: Can they?

463
00:36:46,713 --> 00:36:47,117
Scott: I don't know.

464
00:36:47,380 --> 00:36:48,369
Scott: Do you have to pay for that?

465
00:36:49,915 --> 00:36:50,870
Scott: It was really interesting.

466
00:36:51,070 --> 00:36:52,890
Scott: But hey, it's pretty great also.

467
00:36:53,270 --> 00:36:53,370
Scott: Yeah.

468
00:36:53,490 --> 00:36:53,776
Scott: I like it.

469
00:36:54,750 --> 00:37:05,130
Rym: And, you know, it's generally when something goes completely dialogue free, that is the longer the movie is, the harder it is to pull that off without the thing dragging.

470
00:37:05,790 --> 00:37:06,191
Rym: Like that is.

471
00:37:06,572 --> 00:37:12,629
Rym: that is an artistic feat to be able to make a movie this long that is purely dialogue free and make it so compelling the whole way.

472
00:37:13,393 --> 00:37:15,590
Scott: I mean, what used to be you had to make a silent movie.

473
00:37:15,752 --> 00:37:16,626
Scott: You couldn't make a talkie.

474
00:37:16,990 --> 00:37:17,170
Rym: Yeah.

475
00:37:17,771 --> 00:37:26,530
Scott: And then once you could make a talkie, it's like, oh, finally we're fucking silent movies and silent movies are really rare kind of, you know, film school things or whatever.

476
00:37:26,830 --> 00:37:32,570
Scott: It's like, you know, sort of a niche cultural, you know, kind of kind of thing, right?

477
00:37:32,630 --> 00:37:39,390
Scott: No one is like, I can't remember any silent movie that was like in theaters, you know, any time recently.

478
00:37:39,992 --> 00:37:44,610
Scott: So this isn't a silent movie because there is plenty of audio all the time.

479
00:37:45,151 --> 00:37:50,010
Scott: But there is no spoken dialogue, which, you know, a really makes it great for a children's film.

480
00:37:50,651 --> 00:37:55,930
Scott: But I think there were a lot of things that in the movie are written down in English text.

481
00:37:56,590 --> 00:38:05,510
Scott: So someone near me because it's Children's Film Festival was basically any time there was text on the screen was telling their child what you know, what it said.

482
00:38:06,193 --> 00:38:08,929
Scott: And that makes it less of a children's movie, I think.

483
00:38:09,490 --> 00:38:09,591
Scott: Right.

484
00:38:09,611 --> 00:38:11,570
Scott: That you have to read, you know, children.

485
00:38:11,870 --> 00:38:12,591
Rym: Like the postcard from Duck.

486
00:38:12,631 --> 00:38:22,830
Rym: Like if you can't read what that postcard says, I guess, you know, actually you'd be fine because you'd still know how that went down because there's a picture of a clearly not American place.

487
00:38:23,295 --> 00:38:24,389
Rym: And then dog is sad.

488
00:38:25,470 --> 00:38:33,310
Scott: Yeah, but it's still not, you know, text is less accessible than spoken language to the children.

489
00:38:34,238 --> 00:38:35,409
Scott: At least if the children can hear.

490
00:38:37,631 --> 00:38:47,750
Rym: But aside from being a period piece to like the setting of New York, it was also very much a period piece to the vibe of the pre-internet, pre-cell phone era.

491
00:38:47,870 --> 00:38:58,710
Rym: Because this era of New York, like this era of the world, at least the industrialized world, the late 70s through the 90s was where technology had advanced a lot.

492
00:38:59,111 --> 00:39:07,670
Rym: And the super modern world was fully in effect, except we still didn't have smartphones and like portable devices.

493
00:39:08,150 --> 00:39:14,050
Rym: So how adults make friends, how adults meet people changed so radically after that.

494
00:39:14,451 --> 00:39:23,870
Rym: And I think a lot of younger people won't quite, I think they might experience or at least feel what it was like to interact with people as adults before smartphones.

495
00:39:24,151 --> 00:39:28,730
Rym: Like literally, if your friend doesn't pick up the phone and you don't know where they live, there's no way to find them.

496
00:39:29,531 --> 00:39:30,293
Rym: Like that's it.

497
00:39:30,934 --> 00:39:34,625
Rym: Like looking someone's name up in the phone book to try to get a hold of them.

498
00:39:34,885 --> 00:39:36,570
Rym: That's not a thing anyone does anymore.

499
00:39:38,790 --> 00:39:38,950
Scott: Yep.

500
00:39:39,471 --> 00:39:49,710
Scott: I think also, despite being, you know, old in that way, it's also relevant to the present because I think despite having cell phones, everyone has a smartphone, you know, or at least a lot of people smartphones.

501
00:39:50,110 --> 00:39:51,549
Scott: People are still crazy lonely.

502
00:39:51,811 --> 00:39:52,033
Rym: Yep.

503
00:39:52,355 --> 00:39:54,550
Rym: Oh, there's perhaps even more lonely.

504
00:39:54,710 --> 00:40:04,430
Rym: The US government, there's a report they just issued where they've identified, they're saying that loneliness among adults is getting to the point of being a national security and a national health crisis.

505
00:40:05,130 --> 00:40:05,211
Scott: Yeah.

506
00:40:05,231 --> 00:40:07,298
Scott: I mean, you're talking about people getting robot friends.

507
00:40:07,358 --> 00:40:10,549
Scott: It's like, yeah, I think people are getting robot friends now, right?

508
00:40:11,311 --> 00:40:15,500
Scott: They imagine a robot friend of the eighties is like a thing that didn't actually happen.

509
00:40:15,900 --> 00:40:17,844
Scott: where it's like, it's 2024.

510
00:40:17,844 --> 00:40:19,768
Scott: People are getting robot friends now.

511
00:40:19,868 --> 00:40:20,670
Scott: They're that lonely.

512
00:40:22,471 --> 00:40:30,409
Scott: So it's interesting to sort of take a problem of the present and put it in the past, you know, and I look at it in a difference.

513
00:40:32,011 --> 00:40:42,550
Rym: And in terms of the movie itself, like it really deals with not just adult friendship and relationships, but the like the nature of those without spoiling anything, like just go watch this movie, obviously.

514
00:40:42,790 --> 00:40:51,010
Rym: But like some people at certain stages in their lives are better or worse friends for other people, depending on the stages they're in in their lives.

515
00:40:51,070 --> 00:40:57,021
Rym: Like Dog, not a great friend to Robot, considering Robot's particular needs.

516
00:40:57,562 --> 00:41:01,690
Rym: Dog gives up pretty quick on trying to get Robot out of its situation.

517
00:41:02,470 --> 00:41:09,090
Scott: I think they do a pretty, you know, I think they did try a lot, but they, you know, basically could have tried harder.

518
00:41:09,770 --> 00:41:09,951
Rym: Yeah.

519
00:41:10,071 --> 00:41:12,018
Rym: Well, because Monkey was able to get in there just fine.

520
00:41:13,524 --> 00:41:15,190
Rym: So was Rabbit, Rabbit and Rabbit.

521
00:41:16,157 --> 00:41:16,589
Scott: And birds.

522
00:41:16,911 --> 00:41:17,193
Rym: Yup.

523
00:41:17,454 --> 00:41:21,170
Rym: Granted, Rabbit, Rabbit, Rabbit, as well as Monkey were bad people.

524
00:41:22,156 --> 00:41:22,387
Scott: Sure.

525
00:41:24,951 --> 00:41:31,530
Rym: But also about how people, when they separate, whether intentional or not, might change and become different.

526
00:41:31,790 --> 00:41:42,010
Rym: And how just because you had a good relationship or good memories of the past, you can hold on to those even if you were in a different place and your lives have diverged since then.

527
00:41:42,612 --> 00:41:46,108
Rym: All those themes are woven into this movie, I would say, expertly.

528
00:41:47,690 --> 00:41:50,835
Scott: So the movie did get nominated for Oscar.

529
00:41:51,476 --> 00:42:00,790
Scott: I think, you know, they gave the Oscar to Miyazaki, which I think they would have just given it to him, even if he had put up two hours of just a white screen.

530
00:42:01,412 --> 00:42:03,169
Scott: Because the people who vote don't watch it.

531
00:42:05,074 --> 00:42:07,970
Rym: I will admit, I did not realize the Oscars already happened for some reason.

532
00:42:08,476 --> 00:42:09,190
Scott: They just happened.

533
00:42:09,190 --> 00:42:11,269
Rym: For some reason, I thought they were like in a couple of weeks.

534
00:42:12,752 --> 00:42:14,869
Scott: In my opinion, Spider-Man should have won, but you know.

535
00:42:15,210 --> 00:42:22,650
Rym: I mean, Spider-Man 2 is the best movie I have seen since Spider-Man and Spider-Man is the best movie I've seen since Terminator 2.

536
00:42:22,650 --> 00:42:23,253
Scott: I'm just saying.

537
00:42:23,494 --> 00:42:26,630
Scott: So that's my opinion anyway.

538
00:42:28,031 --> 00:42:35,649
Scott: But, you know, Robot Dreams was definitely, I think, you know, I would put it up, you know, if I had to rank those Oscar nominees, I would put it again above Miyazaki.

539
00:42:37,233 --> 00:42:37,645
Rym: I would too.

540
00:42:38,613 --> 00:42:40,722
Scott: Not to say The Boy and the Heron is bad.

541
00:42:40,742 --> 00:42:42,610
Scott: It's just, you know.

542
00:42:43,130 --> 00:42:57,430
Rym: The Boy and the Heron is a challenging movie that requires a lot of the viewer and is not necessarily what the average person wants or expects when they hear, "I'm gonna go see a Ghibli

543
00:42:57,450 --> 00:42:57,770
Rym: movie.".

544
00:43:00,011 --> 00:43:10,210
Scott: So yeah, the other movie we're gonna see at the film festival on Saturday is the Concierge, which is an anime film from Netflix, which they're bringing over.

545
00:43:11,090 --> 00:43:14,290
Scott: And the thing about it is that it's quite short, actually.

546
00:43:14,370 --> 00:43:18,770
Scott: I think it's only maybe like maybe an hour and not even, I don't know.

547
00:43:19,091 --> 00:43:23,610
Scott: It's not a long one, but it's basically about, it's one of those sort of cute anime ones.

548
00:43:23,670 --> 00:43:27,189
Scott: I guess there must be a thing with concierges now because we just had Pokemon Concierge.

549
00:43:28,013 --> 00:43:29,538
Rym: I was about to say, where's the trifecta?

550
00:43:29,618 --> 00:43:32,870
Rym: If a third concierge thing happens, then we know it's a thing.

551
00:43:33,610 --> 00:43:36,490
Scott: Yeah, it's gonna be like zombies, pirates, ninjas, concierges.

552
00:43:38,671 --> 00:43:47,050
Scott: But basically what it's about is a human woman is a concierge in like some sort of shopping mall, department store kind of mall, hotel place.

553
00:43:47,130 --> 00:43:52,630
Rym: You know, that's another weird similarity because Pokemon Concierge is a human woman is a concierge at a resort for Pokemon.

554
00:43:52,630 --> 00:43:56,430
Scott: And all the customers are like animal people, right?

555
00:43:57,012 --> 00:43:59,303
Scott: So we're gonna see what that's like.

556
00:43:59,343 --> 00:44:00,710
Scott: It should be, you know, cute.

557
00:44:00,951 --> 00:44:02,330
Scott: I don't think it'll be Robot Dreams good.

558
00:44:04,132 --> 00:44:13,910
Rym: I mean, Robot Dreams was just one of the rare confluences of it tried to do something that's somewhat difficult to do and it executed fully on every aspect of that thing.

559
00:44:15,512 --> 00:44:22,230
Scott: I think it's cool that these people who, just some animators over in Europe, Spain, it's like, you know, the dark times happened.

560
00:44:22,710 --> 00:44:29,490
Scott: And so they picked something and just started animating and it turned into, they got an Oscar nomination doing that.

561
00:44:30,011 --> 00:44:33,970
Scott: So hey, artists out there, that's an example to follow.

562
00:44:39,710 --> 00:44:42,080
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

